WEBVTT

1
00:00:13.302 --> 00:00:18.005
By the next morning, November 18, I had recovered from our adventures of the previous day.

2
00:00:18.945 --> 00:00:23.688
I went up on the platform, where Captain Nemo soon appeared and began a series of observations with

3
00:00:23.728 --> 00:00:24.289
his instruments.

4
00:00:25.249 --> 00:00:29.532
Meanwhile some sailors came up to haul in the fishing nets they'd laid the previous night.

5
00:00:30.388 --> 00:00:36.750
Although they appeared to be Europeans, they all used that strange language, so I couldn't determine their nationalities.

6
00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:39.411
Nemo suddenly began speaking to me.

7
00:00:39.431 --> 00:00:42.812
"Professor, isn't this ocean like a living being?

8
00:00:43.832 --> 00:00:44.752
It has its moods.

9
00:00:45.493 --> 00:00:49.474
Last night it slept and now it has awoken after a quiet night."

10
00:00:50.494 --> 00:00:53.715
When Captain Nemo spoke like this, he seemed like a different man.

11
00:00:53.735 --> 00:01:01.675
"I can imagine," he went on, "clusters of submarine houses that would rise every morning to breathe like

12
00:01:01.715 --> 00:01:02.275
the Nautilus.

13
00:01:03.896 --> 00:01:06.558
Free towns, independent cities.

14
00:01:07.358 --> 00:01:12.422
Yet who knows whether some tyrant—" Captain Nemo interrupted himself with a violent gesture.

15
00:01:13.122 --> 00:01:16.004
Our conversation was over without my having said a word.

16
00:01:16.924 --> 00:01:21.727
During the weeks that followed, I seldom saw him, and when I did, he felt no need to

17
00:01:21.787 --> 00:01:22.708
explain his absence.

18
00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:28.583
His lieutenant regularly marked the submarine's course on the map, so I always knew where we were.

19
00:01:28.603 --> 00:01:35.526
By November 27 we had traveled 4,860 leagues and reached Hawaii.

20
00:01:36.467 --> 00:01:38.608
On December 1 we crossed the equator.

21
00:01:39.348 --> 00:01:43.811
From December 4 to 11, we traveled an additional 925 leagues.

22
00:01:44.731 --> 00:01:49.194
We passed December 25 without celebrating Christmas, much to Ned's regret.

23
00:01:50.194 --> 00:01:55.261
Early on the morning of January 1, 1868, Conseil joined me on the platform.

24
00:01:56.742 --> 00:01:59.183
"Master, may I wish you a Happy New Year?"

25
00:01:59.623 --> 00:02:00.004
he asked.

26
00:02:00.024 --> 00:02:02.925
"I thank you for your good wishes," I said.

27
00:02:02.945 --> 00:02:07.848
"But I must ask what you mean by a 'happy' year in this situation.

28
00:02:08.788 --> 00:02:12.390
Do you mean a year that will bring us to the end of our imprisonment or a year

29
00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:15.232
that sees us continue on this strange voyage?"

30
00:02:16.252 --> 00:02:18.213
"I don't really know how to answer, Master.

31
00:02:18.934 --> 00:02:22.496
We're sure to see some interesting things if we continue with Captain Nemo.

32
00:02:23.476 --> 00:02:28.378
In the past two months we haven't had time to be bored; the latest marvel is always the

33
00:02:28.418 --> 00:02:29.198
most astonishing.

34
00:02:30.159 --> 00:02:35.201
So I'd say a happy year would be one where we could see everything—" "Everything, Conseil?

35
00:02:35.961 --> 00:02:37.242
No year could be that long.

36
00:02:37.922 --> 00:02:39.863
But what does Ned Land think about all this?"

37
00:02:39.883 --> 00:02:44.525
"Ned's thoughts are exactly the opposite of mine," said Conseil.

38
00:02:44.545 --> 00:02:50.467
"He's tired of staring at fish through the windows and eating them at every meal."

39
00:02:50.487 --> 00:02:53.308
"Oh, I don't mind the food," I said.

40
00:02:53.328 --> 00:02:56.170
"Neither do I," replied Conseil.

41
00:02:56.190 --> 00:02:59.713
"I think as much about staying as Ned does about escaping.

42
00:03:00.673 --> 00:03:03.775
So no matter what happens, one of us will be happy.

43
00:03:04.816 --> 00:03:08.338
In conclusion I wish for you to have whatever will make you happy."

44
00:03:09.359 --> 00:03:12.261
We shook hands and Conseil descended into the submarine.

45
00:03:13.162 --> 00:03:18.745
By the next day, January 2, we had traveled 5,250 leagues under the sea.

46
00:03:19.786 --> 00:03:25.674
Over the next two days, we crossed the Coral Sea, and on January 4, we sighted the coast

47
00:03:25.794 --> 00:03:26.315
of New Guinea.

48
00:03:27.296 --> 00:03:32.520
On that day Captain Nemo announced that he intended to get to the Indian Ocean by the Torres

49
00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:34.722
Strait between New Guinea and Australia.

50
00:03:36.243 --> 00:03:42.308
Filled with coral reefs, it was a route that even the boldest navigators feared, and many considered the

51
00:03:42.348 --> 00:03:44.070
world's most dangerous strait.

52
00:03:45.050 --> 00:03:51.436
The passage is about seventy miles wide, but it's obstructed by countless islands, islets, and rocks.

53
00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:57.163
It's almost impossible to navigate, so Captain Nemo had to take every precaution.

54
00:03:58.263 --> 00:04:01.945
Rising to the surface, the Nautilus proceeded slowly through the water.

55
00:04:02.906 --> 00:04:07.928
My two companions and I took advantage of the situation and climbed to the deserted platform.

56
00:04:08.909 --> 00:04:11.650
I suspected that Captain Nemo was piloting the sub himself.

57
00:04:12.491 --> 00:04:16.693
Around the Nautilus the sea appeared to boil as waves broke over the coral.

58
00:04:16.713 --> 00:04:19.655
"This is a rough sea," said Ned.

59
00:04:19.675 --> 00:04:21.896
"I hope the captain knows what he's doing."

60
00:04:22.888 --> 00:04:28.351
Indeed the situation was dangerous, but the Nautilus seemed to glide through the water like magic.

61
00:04:29.812 --> 00:04:34.735
At about three in the afternoon, we were only a couple miles away from the island of Gilboa.

62
00:04:35.695 --> 00:04:38.697
Suddenly I was knocked off my feet—we had run aground!

63
00:04:39.658 --> 00:04:45.361
The Nautilus had just touched the coral below, but the vessel was now listing slightly to its portside.

64
00:04:46.362 --> 00:04:50.144
When I stood up, I saw that Captain Nemo and his lieutenant were on the platform.

65
00:04:51.148 --> 00:04:55.291
They were examining the situation and talking in their incomprehensible language.

66
00:04:56.332 --> 00:05:01.076
Unfortunately we had run aground in a part of the ocean where the tides were not strong.

67
00:05:01.996 --> 00:05:05.919
It would be difficult for the tide to rise high enough to float the sub off the coral.

68
00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:11.384
I was thinking about this when the captain, cool and calm as ever, approached me.

69
00:05:11.404 --> 00:05:12.945
"An accident?"

70
00:05:13.305 --> 00:05:13.705
I asked.

71
00:05:13.725 --> 00:05:16.768
"No, an incident," he replied.

72
00:05:17.708 --> 00:05:21.809
"But an incident that may oblige you to become an inhabitant of land once more?"

73
00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:27.271
Captain Nemo shook his head as if to say that nothing could force him to set foot on

74
00:05:27.311 --> 00:05:28.031
dry land again.

75
00:05:28.051 --> 00:05:31.332
"The Nautilus is not damaged, Professor.

76
00:05:32.192 --> 00:05:35.973
It will still carry you into the midst of the ocean's marvels," he replied.

77
00:05:35.993 --> 00:05:42.435
"Our voyage has only begun, and I would not want to be deprived so soon of the honor

78
00:05:42.455 --> 00:05:43.095
of your company."

79
00:05:43.115 --> 00:05:46.336
"But the tides aren't very high here," I said.

80
00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:52.221
"That is true, but in five days, the moon will be full, so the tide will be at

81
00:05:52.261 --> 00:05:52.822
its highest.

82
00:05:53.842 --> 00:05:56.863
I fully expect the moon to take care of this incident for me."

83
00:05:57.843 --> 00:06:02.224
Having said that, Captain Nemo and his lieutenant went back into the interior of the submarine.

84
00:06:03.264 --> 00:06:05.925
Once they were gone, Ned and Conseil approached me.

85
00:06:05.945 --> 00:06:07.786
"Well, sir?"

86
00:06:08.206 --> 00:06:08.666
asked Ned.

87
00:06:08.686 --> 00:06:11.247
"What did Captain Nemo have to say?"

88
00:06:12.327 --> 00:06:16.008
I explained how the captain said we must wait for the full moon to do its work.

89
00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:19.082
"Really?" said Ned skeptically.

90
00:06:19.102 --> 00:06:21.083
"We're just going to sit here?"

91
00:06:21.103 --> 00:06:25.506
"The captain is not going to do anything to try and move the Nautilus?"

92
00:06:25.967 --> 00:06:26.627
asked Conseil.

93
00:06:26.647 --> 00:06:29.089
"That is correct," I said.

94
00:06:30.070 --> 00:06:32.331
Ned looked at Conseil and then shrugged his shoulders.

95
00:06:32.351 --> 00:06:37.755
"Sir, believe me, this hunk of metal is never going to navigate the sea again.

96
00:06:38.816 --> 00:06:43.400
It's only fit to be sold for scrap, therefore, I think it's time to part company with Captain

97
00:06:43.420 --> 00:06:48.634
Nemo." I still had faith in the captain and his vessel, so I counseled Ned to stay put.

98
00:06:48.654 --> 00:06:54.476
"Flight might be possible if we were in sight of the English or French coasts, but out here—"

99
00:06:54.496 --> 00:06:57.337
"Couldn't we at least go ashore?"

100
00:06:57.898 --> 00:06:58.358
asked Ned.

101
00:06:58.378 --> 00:07:03.700
"There are likely to be animals roaming the island, and I'm sure they could be turned into fine

102
00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:04.741
roasts and cutlets."

103
00:07:05.781 --> 00:07:08.522
"Master, I agree with Ned," said Conseil.

104
00:07:08.542 --> 00:07:15.173
"Couldn't you ask your friend, Captain Nemo, to put us ashore, if only so we don't forget how

105
00:07:15.213 --> 00:07:16.214
to walk on land?"

106
00:07:16.234 --> 00:07:19.456
"I'll ask, but I know he'll refuse."

107
00:07:20.457 --> 00:07:23.498
To my great surprise, the captain agreed to our request.

108
00:07:24.479 --> 00:07:30.222
He didn't even make us promise to return, probably knowing that fleeing across New Guinea would be perilous.

109
00:07:31.263 --> 00:07:35.886
I'd decided that if Ned asked me about escaping, I'd say it was better to be a prisoner

110
00:07:35.966 --> 00:07:39.148
onboard the Nautilus than to fall into the hands of the natives.
